The flush handover: making buffer → holding atomic to a reader
2026-08-10 · single node, laptop scale (bench/compose/timelakedb.yml)
================================================================================

FOUND WHILE PREPARING P0-1, not while looking for it. The CI work needed
`cargo test --workspace` to be trustworthy on a cold runner, and
`tests/health.rs::rows_stay_visible_while_a_slow_flush_uploads` was not: it
passed when the test binary ran whole and failed when run alone, and it
failed once under heavy load in the full suite. A test that depends on
timing is not a flaky test to be re-run — it is a race in the code it
tests, reported honestly.

THE RACE. A read observes rows in three places, in order: the live buffer,
the holding area for rows mid-flush, then the catalog's files. A flush
moved rows out of the buffer and into holding as two separately-locked
steps, so a reader could land between them and see the rows in NEITHER —
not in the buffer they had left, not yet in holding, and not in the catalog
until the commit some 300 ms later. That is a *vanish*: an acknowledged row
reported as missing. It is the one failure this database refuses, and it
was reachable by a plain COUNT(*) at the wrong moment.

Reproduced deterministically with a Store that sleeps 150 ms per put (the
shape of an object store), which widens the window enough to hit reliably:
before the fix, the isolated test failed every run with `table not found`.

THE WRONG FIX, RECORDED BECAUSE IT MEASURED WORSE. Holding the holding-area
write lock across the swap closes the vanish — a reader that got past the
buffer simply waits and then sees the batch. But a reader that had ALREADY
read the buffer now waits and then counts the same rows a second time. The
vanish became a duplicate, 4 runs in 6:

    assertion `left == right` failed: acked rows vanished mid-flush
      left: Some(6)    right: Some(3)

Both defects are the same bug seen from two sides: the reader takes two
independent observations, and a flush between them is visible.

THE FIX. One critical section for the whole handover — `ingest_gate`,
`dbs`, `flushing` — and every reader takes the same locks in the same
order, holding both while it looks at buffer and holding area together. A
query then observes the rows in exactly one place: never neither, never
both. Three read sites were changed to match (`sql_batches`, `live_tables`,
`snapshot_ipc` — the last two are what a CL-3 querier calls, so the same
defect was reachable across the cluster wire).

    isolated test, 5 runs      PASS (had never passed before)
    full health binary, 6 runs PASS, neither failure mode
    workspace, 173 tests       PASS
    2-core container, 3 runs   PASS

WHAT IT COSTS, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSERTED. Building the Arrow snapshot
now happens under the ingest gate, so writers pause for it instead of only
for the pointer swap. Laptop scale, clean volume each time, 20 s warm-up:

    before (HEAD, no fix)   604,337 · 605,860 · 588,870 lines/s   ~600k
    after  (this change)    571,021 · 570,969 lines/s             ~571k

**≈5% of ingest throughput.** Exactness at the price of 5% is the trade
this project has made everywhere else, and it is stated here rather than
buried. Two further readings from the same runs: `rows_48h` = 3,756,632
against 3,756,634 written — the −2 is LWW dedup of source primary-key
collisions, matching the −8 the M3 laptop baseline recorded on identical
data — and all five Shape B queries completed, Shape A median 64 ms.

A CHEAPER DESIGN EXISTS, and is deliberately not taken tonight: move the
`TableBuffer` itself into the holding area rather than a snapshot of it,
making the swap a pointer move and leaving readers to snapshot it as they
already snapshot the live buffer. That removes the write-path cost
entirely. It changes the type the read path and the CL-3 wire both consume,
which is not a change to make at the end of an evening — recorded as the
follow-up rather than half-done.

TWO OUTLIERS IN THE RECORDED RUNS, both explained rather than dropped:
  - 230,294 and 233,099 lines/s appear in the *pre-fix* and *post-fix* sets
    alike. Both are the first run against a newly created Docker volume
    after only a ~10 s wait; with a 20–25 s warm-up they do not recur. They
    measure Docker Desktop, not the database.
  - `timelakedb-p01-flush-atomicity-*` reports `rows_48h` = 3,831,020,
    a 74,386-row surplus. That run reused a volume still holding an earlier
    smoke run's rows. Kept as a caution: `docker compose down` does not
    remove a named volume, and an exactness number from a dirty volume
    means nothing.
